\section*{Borsuk's Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Nine-dimensional diameter covering}: The projector configuration of lines in $\mathbb R^4$, viewed in the nine-dimensional space of trace-one symmetric matrices, is claimed to require more than ten smaller-diameter covering sets. This compact configuration refutes Borsuk’s assertion in dimension nine.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Nine-dimensional diameter covering}: A smaller-diameter cover is enlarged to an open cover and converted into nonnegative coordinate functions on projective three-space. Orthogonal lines must have disjoint positive supports. Extending the map to positive semidefinite matrices produces an odd sphere map; its mod-two degree and an equality analysis of the support complex contradict the possible ten-label structure.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Nine-dimensional diameter covering}: The nine-dimensional bounded rank-one-projector example needs more than ten smaller-diameter sets, a direct counterexample to the universally quantified Borsuk assertion.

The claimed counterexample lies within the question’s hypotheses and contradicts its universal conclusion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 156 describes Lean coverage for the nine-dimensional projector covering obstruction against ten arbitrary smaller-diameter sets.

\textit{Nine-dimensional diameter covering}: The manuscript links \texttt{BorsukNine.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.BorsukNine.main\_theorem}).

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

